is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Inter-VLAN routing between VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 in Location A is operating correctly at time of test. The router-on-a-stick termination on router0 shows both subinterfaces up with correct addressing: eth0.10 = 172.16.10.1/24 and eth0.20 = 172.16.20.1/24. PC4 (172.16.10.4/24, default via 172.16.10.1) can reach both its own gateway and the VLAN 20 gateway (172.16.20.1) with 0% loss. End-to-end inter-VLAN connectivity is verified: PC4 → Laptop0 ping succeeds (3/3, ~1.4 ms, TTL=63), and Laptop0 → PC4 ping succeeds (3/3, ~1.1 ms, TTL=63). This demonstrates Layer-3 forwarding on router0 and correct 802.1Q tagging across the mswitch1 trunk to switch1 in both directions.

Mechanism: Traffic from VLAN 10 is tagged to router0.eth0.10, routed to eth0.20, and returned over the trunk to VLAN 20. The successful pings and gateway reachability confirm the encapsulation and routing path.

Localization: Router0 subinterfaces eth0.10 and eth0.20, trunk link between router0 and mswitch1, and access ports toward switch1/hosts.

Root cause: No misconfiguration found; inter-VLAN routing is healthy.

Corrective fix: None required. If the user experienced an intermittent issue, consider checking for transient switchport flaps or prior changes; otherwise, close the ticket as not reproducible with current configuration and state. === END ===